1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:05,077
A workflow coordinates tasks, decisions,
and checks in a defined order.

2
00:00:05,257 --> 00:00:10,359
In this synthetic example, the agent can
choose how to inspect the quantity code

3
00:00:10,359 --> 00:00:15,143
and propose a small fix, while specific
tests measure what the change does.

4
00:00:15,323 --> 00:00:18,864
Start by mapping the steps from issue to
pull request.

5
00:00:19,044 --> 00:00:23,289
Mark where the workflow waits for a person
or for an objective check.

6
00:00:24,757 --> 00:00:28,629
The training issue says that orders accept
positive quantities.

7
00:00:28,809 --> 00:00:33,629
Put the four example cases into the test
plan before asking for a code change.

8
00:00:33,809 --> 00:00:39,137
A deterministic unit test uses a fixed
input and expected result, so you can

9
00:00:39,137 --> 00:00:41,170
repeat it after another edit.

10
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:46,481
A short table also makes the requirement
easier for a reviewer to evaluate.

11
00:00:47,923 --> 00:00:52,438
In the approved environment, add the
regression test for zero and run it

12
00:00:52,438 --> 00:00:54,695
against the original implementation.

13
00:00:54,875 --> 00:00:57,917
The example fails with the expected
assertion.

14
00:00:58,097 --> 00:01:03,695
Read the actual failure, because an import
error or an unrelated assertion would not

15
00:01:03,695 --> 00:01:05,095
demonstrate this bug.

16
00:01:05,275 --> 00:01:08,625
Save the output and the source revision
that produced it.

17
00:01:08,805 --> 00:01:12,173
Now the author can ask the agent for the
minimal fix.

18
00:01:13,632 --> 00:01:16,492
At this point, the workflow has one fixed
gate.

19
00:01:16,672 --> 00:01:21,888
After the change, advance only when the
required test command exits successfully

20
00:01:21,888 --> 00:01:23,713
and the diff fits the issue.

21
00:01:23,893 --> 00:01:26,852
A failure should lead to inspection of the
output.

22
00:01:27,032 --> 00:01:32,525
Keep the regression test unchanged unless
a person approves a correction to the test

23
00:01:32,525 --> 00:01:32,983
itself.

24
00:01:33,163 --> 00:01:36,472
This way the evidence stays comparable
across attempts.

25
00:01:37,923 --> 00:01:41,730
The example uses the same command after
the code change.

26
00:01:41,910 --> 00:01:45,792
The regression now passes, and the
previous cases still pass.

27
00:01:45,972 --> 00:01:49,565
Verify the exact revision and check the
diff yourself.

28
00:01:49,745 --> 00:01:52,990
The passing run establishes the tested
behavior.

29
00:01:53,170 --> 00:01:58,286
The human review still checks whether the
source is clear, in scope, and appropriate

30
00:01:58,286 --> 00:01:59,443
for the repository.

31
00:02:00,923 --> 00:02:05,798
After the human author opens the training
pull request, inspect its configured

32
00:02:05,798 --> 00:02:08,048
checks from the authorized terminal.

33
00:02:08,228 --> 00:02:13,241
Continuous integration should run the
relevant tests for the proposed revision.

34
00:02:13,421 --> 00:02:18,512
Check names, elapsed time, and pending
status belong to this repository's

35
00:02:18,512 --> 00:02:19,489
configuration.

36
00:02:19,669 --> 00:02:24,881
If a required check is pending or failed,
wait for evidence or address the failure

37
00:02:24,881 --> 00:02:26,025
before proceeding.

38
00:02:27,507 --> 00:02:32,238
Give the reviewer the issue, the test
command, the failing and passing outcomes,

39
00:02:32,238 --> 00:02:33,361
and the final diff.

40
00:02:33,541 --> 00:02:39,017
The reviewer uses their own account and
GitHub CLI to verify the details and

41
00:02:39,017 --> 00:02:40,602
record their decision.

42
00:02:40,782 --> 00:02:46,036
This workflow stays reliable because each
step leads to a visible result, and the

43
00:02:46,036 --> 00:02:50,317
handoff depends on concrete evidence
rather than an agent's claim.
